WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.162
Numbers can come together.
Numbers can go away.

2
00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:12.793
Today you will add and take away.
You will also make pairs of ten.

3
00:00:13.293 --> 00:00:17.055
Adding means putting two groups
together. Then you count them all.

4
00:00:17.275 --> 00:00:21.176
Here are three counters.
Here are two more counters.

5
00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:24.925
Push the two groups together.
Then count every counter.

6
00:00:25.145 --> 00:00:28.628
One, two, three, four, five.
So three and two make five.

7
00:00:28.848 --> 00:00:31.496
Or start at three and
count on: four, five.

8
00:00:31.996 --> 00:00:35.479
Taking away starts with
one group. Some of it goes.

9
00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:39.135
Here are seven counters.
Now take three of them away.

10
00:00:39.355 --> 00:00:42.513
Those three have gone.
Count the ones still here.

11
00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:46.541
Four are left. So seven
take away three is four.

12
00:00:46.761 --> 00:00:50.616
Counting back works too. Start
at seven: six, five, four.

13
00:00:51.116 --> 00:00:55.667
How many more is a taking away
question. It is not an adding one.

14
00:00:55.887 --> 00:00:59.973
Here are eight red counters.
Here are five blue ones.

15
00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:03.351
Match them up in pairs.
You can make five pairs.

16
00:01:03.571 --> 00:01:07.751
Three red ones have no partner.
So eight is three more than five.

17
00:01:08.251 --> 00:01:12.291
Two numbers can add up to ten.
That pair is a number bond.

18
00:01:12.511 --> 00:01:16.041
Here are ten counters in a
row. Split them after six.

19
00:01:16.261 --> 00:01:20.208
Six on this side, four on that
side. Six and four make ten.

20
00:01:20.428 --> 00:01:26.419
Seven counters leave three empty
spaces. Seven and three make ten.

21
00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:30.820
A double is the same amount
again. It happens on both sides.

22
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.640
This ladybird has three
spots on each side.

23
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.993
Three spots and three more
spots. Count them all: six.

24
00:01:38.213 --> 00:01:41.139
So double three is six. It is not four.

25
00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:45.957
Double seven fills one ten frame,
with four left over. Fourteen.

26
00:01:46.457 --> 00:01:50.033
We count our money in
shillings. We write it as sh.

27
00:01:50.253 --> 00:01:53.875
Every coin has a number on
it. That is what it is worth.

28
00:01:54.095 --> 00:01:57.485
The price is seven shillings.
Start with a five coin.

29
00:01:57.705 --> 00:02:01.049
Now add a two coin. Five and
two make seven shillings.

30
00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:05.821
Adding puts two groups together.
Taking away leaves fewer.

31
00:02:06.041 --> 00:02:09.989
How many more finds the gap.
And six and four make ten.

32
00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:13.692
Double three is six. A coin
is worth its own number.

33
00:02:13.912 --> 00:02:17.302
You can add and take away.
You can go up to twenty.
